cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you refer to this evaluation, please cite it as below."
title: "Jev on a synthetic survey: does a native probability vector beat a verbalized one?"
abstract: >-
  A comparison of a decision-only model that returns a probability vector natively
  (TypeSafe Jev 1.13.0) against gpt-4.1 asked to state one, over 300 respondents and 108
  questions of the Twin-2K-500 synthetic-survey benchmark. Asked each yes/no question as a
  single probability (a Noul), Jev leads gpt-4.1's stated probabilities on all six reported
  measures at about a thirty-fourth of the cost at list rates. Asked as a two-option choice, the
  comparison was first set up around, it trails on the distribution gap, and no arm met the
  calibration bar fixed in advance. How the question was asked moved the result more than
  which model was used. Independent work: the author has no affiliation with TypeSafe or
  OpenAI and paid for all model usage.
type: dataset
repository-code: "https://github.com/jjd-lab/jev-synthetic-survey"
